WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.560
In an earlier lesson you met rhodopsin.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.080
It has seven helices holding retinal.

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:16.020
Retinal changes shape when it absorbs a photon, and that sets off a G protein.

4
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.240
Some microbes have opsins on a similar plan that skip the relay.

5
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.220
Channelrhodopsin-2, from a green alga, is itself an ion channel.

6
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:31.493
Now shine blue light on channelrhodopsin-2.

7
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.273
Blue light changes its retinal.

8
00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:37.113
The pore opens within milliseconds.

9
00:00:38.273 --> 00:00:42.073
Cations flow into the neuron, with little choice among them.

10
00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:44.413
That depolarizes the membrane.

11
00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:47.233
At threshold, the neuron fires.

12
00:00:48.216 --> 00:00:56.256
Georg Nagel, Peter Hegemann, Ernst Bamberg and colleagues had shown in 2002 and 2003

13
00:00:56.496 --> 00:01:02.196
that channelrhodopsins from the green alga Chlamydomonas are light-gated channels.

14
00:01:03.276 --> 00:01:09.996
The same work showed that channelrhodopsin-2 passes cations, and that it works in mammalian cells.

16
00:01:10.816 --> 00:01:19.476
In 2005 Edward Boyden, Feng Zhang, Ernst Bamberg, Georg Nagel and Karl Deisseroth

17
00:01:19.476 --> 00:01:22.556
reported on hippocampal neurons in culture.

18
00:01:23.836 --> 00:01:29.536
Given the gene for channelrhodopsin-2, the neurons fired action potentials in step

19
00:01:29.536 --> 00:01:31.856
with millisecond flashes of blue light.

20
00:01:32.976 --> 00:01:36.616
Other laboratories reported similar results at about the same time.
